// A typed dereference inside print().
//
// This program was a compile error until v1.2.0. The parser tracked whether it
// was inside a `print(...)` call and rejected `?p |> Type` there outright, with
// "typed dereference cannot be used inside print() — assign to a variable
// first". That made the grammar context-sensitive: the same expression was
// legal or illegal depending on the name of the call it sat in.
//
// The rule it was protecting is real. A bare `?p` streams tokens as they
// arrive, and a typed deref cannot, because the value does not exist until
// generation finishes and coercion runs. But that is a difference in *when*
// print receives its argument, not a reason to reject the program. print now
// takes either and does the right thing with each.

prompt p = "What is 3 + 4? Respond with only the number."

// Not streamed: the int arrives complete, then prints.
print(?p |> int)

// Still streamed, exactly as before: no type stage, so tokens appear live.
print(?p)
